00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:13,890
In the Encyclopedia of Triangle Centers, two classical circles are constructed directly on segments between Collection One centers: the Brocard Circle with diameter X three X six, and the Orthocentroidal Circle with diameter X two X four.

2
00:00:14,310 --> 00:00:23,899
The Brocard circle passes through the Circumcenter X three, the Symmedian Point X six, the two Brocard points, and the vertices of the First and Second Brocard triangles.

3
00:00:23,899 --> 00:00:30,160
The Orthocentroidal circle has diameter from the Centroid X two to the Orthocenter X four along the Euler line.

4
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:43,500
By Andrew Guinand's nineteen eighty-four theorem, the Incenter X one always lies strictly inside the open orthocentroidal disk—except in an equilateral triangle—revealing the deep metric boundary governed by X two and X four.
